=== WHAT THIS RUN SURFACED =============================================
  1. AN IMAGE DOCUMENT IS COLUMN-HUNGRIER THAN THE TEXT ENGINE'S BUDGET.
     This corpus yields seven columns per image — path, format, orientation, colour
     bucket, width, height, byte length — and `index_text::MAX_FIELD` is 4. Three of
     them could not be indexed at all: orientation kept only as text, and height and
     width have no numeric column, so neither can carry a hard range predicate.
     This is not a bench limitation to route around. `p50` claims one query plan over
     text, facet, numeric, vector and hash predicates; that claim is bounded by how
     many predicates fit. Raising MAX_FIELD is the WRONG fix — it is the width of
     `[u16; MAX_FIELD]` on every posting, so it taxes every text consumer to serve
     this one tier. A candidate roadmap row, recorded rather than papered over.

  2. ON THIS CORPUS THE FILE EXTENSION DOES NOT LIE.
     `meta::sniff` disagreed with the extension on 0 of 17311 files (0.00%).
     That CONTRADICTS the expectation that scraped assets carry lying extensions.
     One Supabase-backed scrape is not the web: this host serves what it stored,
     and the scraper wrote the extension from the URL, so the two agree by
     construction. The sniff is still the right default — this run just does not
     produce evidence for it, and says so instead of quietly implying otherwise.

  3. THE NEAR-DUPLICATE RATE IS A CHOICE, NOT A MEASUREMENT.
     The dHash table above spans thresholds 0..12 and the PDQ table 0..48. Both move
     the answer by tens of percentage points. Any single headline rate for this
     corpus would be the threshold talking, which is exactly §5's warning.

